Here’s a clear, simple explanation of static vs global, especially useful for Java (since you’re a Java developer).

1️⃣ What is Static?
Meaning

static belongs to the class, not to an object.

Only one copy exists in memory, shared by all objects.

Static Variable (Java)
class Employee {
    static int companyCode = 101;
    String name;
}


companyCode → shared

name → per object

Static Method
class MathUtil {
    static int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }
}


Call without object:

MathUtil.add(2, 3);

Static Block
static {
    System.out.println("Loaded once when class loads");
}


Runs once, when class is loaded.

2️⃣ What is Global?
Important Truth

👉 Java has NO global variables like C/C++.

There is no keyword global in Java.

3️⃣ Why People Say “Global” in Java

When people say global variable in Java, they usually mean:

Static Variable (Class-level)
class Config {
    public static String ENV = "PROD";
}


This behaves like a global variable, but it is actually static.

4️⃣ Static vs Global (Comparison Table)
| Feature        | Static (Java) | Global (C/C++) |
| -------------- | ------------- | -------------- |
| Keyword exists | ✅ Yes         | ✅ Yes          |
| Belongs to     | Class         | Entire program |
| Object needed  | ❌ No          | ❌ No           |
| Encapsulation  | ✅ Yes         | ❌ Poor         |
| Thread-safe    | ❌ By default  | ❌              |
| Java support   | ✅             | ❌              |

5️⃣ Example: “Global” Config in Java (Correct Way)
public class AppConfig {
    private AppConfig() {}  // prevent object creation

    public static final String BASE_URL = "https://api.example.com";
}


Usage:

String url = AppConfig.BASE_URL;

6️⃣ Common Interview Question
❓ Is static variable global in Java?

Answer:

No. Java does not support global variables.
Static variables are class-level, not global.

7️⃣ When to Use Static

✔ Constants (static final)
✔ Utility methods
✔ Shared configuration
✔ Counters

8️⃣ When NOT to Use Static

❌ Business state
❌ User-specific data
❌ Request-scoped data
❌ Spring beans state

🟢 One-Line Summary

Java has no global variables. Static variables are often used as a safe, controlled alternative.